1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,479
This build started with a real failure.

2
00:00:02,479 --> 00:00:07,514
The livestream setup burned hours, so the agent turned that incident into the demo itself.

3
00:00:07,514 --> 00:00:17,467
The mission is simple: verify the live stream, ground the work against a canonical GitHub incident, notify the operator through ntfy, and write the verified receipts back to GitHub.

4
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:20,486
But the interesting part is not that it touches three apps.

5
00:00:20,486 --> 00:00:23,033
It is that every action is evidence-bound.

6
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:31,771
The runtime follows one loop: observe, model state, plan, policy check, act, observe again, evaluate, and leave evidence.

7
00:00:31,771 --> 00:00:34,271
Here, YouTube is read first.

8
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:38,611
The agent proves the exact video identity, title, and live state.

9
00:00:38,611 --> 00:00:42,573
Then GitHub issue number one is read as the canonical mission contract.

10
00:00:42,573 --> 00:00:45,769
Before the first external write, the runtime stops.

11
00:00:45,769 --> 00:00:49,448
The exact ntfy payload is hashed and requires approval.

12
00:00:49,448 --> 00:00:54,743
After approval, the notification is sent once and read back from the exact isolated topic.

13
00:00:54,743 --> 00:00:59,012
The real run exposed an eventual-consistency race in that verification path.

14
00:00:59,012 --> 00:01:02,125
Instead of hiding it, the runtime failed closed.

15
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:07,656
We fixed the verifier, reconciled the original receipt, and did not resend the notification.

16
00:01:07,656 --> 00:01:13,257
The second write, the GitHub evidence comment, also required exact approval and exact read-back.

17
00:01:13,257 --> 00:01:27,432
The final independent evaluator scored the live mission one point zero: YouTube identity passed, GitHub issue identity passed, ntfy receipt passed, GitHub evidence receipt passed, and the runtime policy, verification, and grounding checks all passed.

18
00:01:27,432 --> 00:01:38,493
That same build now has seventy-seven passing tests covering lost responses, duplicate markers, wrong issue identity, policy denial, crash recovery, replay, and adversarial cases.

19
00:01:38,493 --> 00:01:42,113
This is not three disconnected API calls wearing a trench coat.

20
00:01:42,113 --> 00:01:48,186
It is a bounded agent that can act, prove what happened, survive uncertainty, and refuse to fake success.

21
00:01:48,186 --> 00:01:54,507
The code, evidence, tests, and build history are public in fR3kdev slash fR3k.

22
00:01:54,507 --> 00:01:57,750
The entire system was built in public during the hackathon.

